<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zoroastrianism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zoroastrians, Zoroastrian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zoroastrianism is an ancient Iranian religion founded on the teachings of the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zoroaster</a>, centred on the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a> and the struggle between good and evil. It was the state religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, whose kings drew legitimacy from it. After the Arab conquest it declined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, though Zoroastrians survived as a minority and established communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and Zoroastrian temples also existed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism.jpg" alt="Zoroastrianism" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faravahar_on_Fire_Temple,_Yazd.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Caucasian Albania was a frontier society between the Roman and Iranian worlds, between the steppe and the Near East, and between Christianity and <b>Zoroastrianism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Jews, Armenians, Assyrians and <b>Zoroastrians</b> formed significant minorities, while the Babi movement of the 1840s was crushed and its founder, the Bab, executed in 1850.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Decline accelerated under Shah Sultan Husayn (r. 1694–1722), early in whose reign the influential cleric Muhammad Baqir Majlesi (d. 1699) promoted pressure on Sunnis, Sufis, Jews, <b>Zoroastrians</b> and Christians that alienated minority communities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Where the Arsacids had ruled through a loose confederacy of semi-autonomous noble houses, Ardashir pursued centralisation, curbing the great clans, reorganising the administration of the Iranian plateau and elevating <b>Zoroastrianism</b> as the state-sponsored religion, supported by a network of royal fire temples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Chang'an's cosmopolitanism in the early and middle Tang had few equals anywhere: Nestorian Christians, <b>Zoroastrians</b>, Manichaeans and Buddhists maintained temples and monasteries in the capital, alongside communities of Muslim merchants, while the state employed Sogdian financiers, Turkic generals, Korean officials and scholars from Japan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Conversion, however, was gradual: in Egypt, Syria, Iraq and Persia, long centres of Christianity and <b>Zoroastrianism</b>, Muslims probably became a majority only over the following centuries, and substantial Christian and Jewish communities survived throughout the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
